# Cross-compile on the builder's native platform: xcaddy honours GOOS/GOARCH,
# so `--platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64` works from one Dockerfile. The only
# step that runs inside the target image is the final stage's `apk upgrade`,
# which needs QEMU for arm64 (release-docker.yml sets it up).
#
# Security pins:
#   - The FLOOR --with lines (crowdsec/pkg/apiclient, x/net/http2, chi/v5,
#     go-jose/v4, aws-sdk-go-v2/service/route53, klauspost/compress --
#     currently the first SIX) are version floors. Keep every floor above the plugin lines, and update this list
#     when one is added. xcaddy runs `go get` once
#     per --with, in order, and `go get pkg@ver` sets exactly that version, so
#     they must come first: a plugin fetched after them can still raise these
#     modules but never lowers them.
#     crowdsec/pkg/apiclient @v1.7.8 is the documented CVE-BEARING minimum
#     (past CVE-2026-44982 and CVE-2026-44981) but is NOT a clean version and
#     is NOT what ships: two CVE-less advisories reach <= 1.7.8 (GHSA-rh69,
#     GHSA-g2x2, both patched 1.8.0) -- see the crowdsec block below, which
#     measures why neither is linked. What protects the image is that MVS
#     resolves higher, not this number: caddy-crowdsec-bouncer @v0.15.0 below
#     requires crowdsec v1.8.1, so MVS resolves to 1.8.1 and this floor is
#     currently slack. It stays as the stated minimum, so a future bouncer
#     wanting an older crowdsec cannot silently drop below it. Check the built
#     binary, not this line, for the version in the image.
#     x/net/http2 @v0.62.0 lifts golang.org/x/net past CVE-2026-78669,
#     CVE-2026-78661 and CVE-2026-97045.
#     chi/v5 @v5.5.0 lifts go-chi past CVE-2026-114437 and CVE-2026-118681
#     (auth bypass in chi middleware.SupressNotFound). It does NOT clear
#     CVE-2026-122132 -- and the reason that is easy to miss is the subject of
#     the chi block below, which is where a reader checking this pin should go.
#     go-jose/v4 @v4.1.7 lifts go-jose past SIX live advisories, not one:
#     CVE-2026-116587 (HIGH, = GHSA-mgcc-cww5-fp2q, fixed in 4.1.6) PLUS five
#     published 2026-10-30 with NO CVE id -- v6h2, c67m, p9j4, r39w, 27pc --
#     each vulnerable at <= v4.1.6 and patched only in v4.1.7. So 4.1.7 is
#     REQUIRED, not currency; 4.1.6 would be insufficient. Those five are
#     absent from OSV and from GitHub's global advisory index (both 404) and
#     are visible only via repos/go-jose/go-jose/security-advisories, because
#     an advisory with no CVE id is not promoted into the global or ecosystem
#     indexes -- the same blind spot documented for crowdsec below. Do not
#     conclude "no advisories" for any pin here from OSV alone (T-0646).
#     go-jose/v4 was ALREADY in this image transitively at v4.1.5, so this pin
#     is a version floor on an existing module, not a new dependency. A
#     production import path exists via smallstep/certificates ->
#     coreos/go-oidc/v3 -> go-jose/v4 (oidc/jose.go, jwks.go, verify.go),
#     reachable only with `acme_server` configured, which no fleet host does.
#     The sibling go-jose/v3 line is now GONE from the binary -- see the
#     --replace block below and T-0645. It previously shipped at v3.0.5 with
#     EIGHT advisories and no v3 fix published at any version.
#     Caddy already links these packages, so the blank import xcaddy adds for
#     each changes nothing else. The build fails if `go get` ever reports a
#     downgrade, so a pin can't quietly pull a plugin back to an older release.
#   - crowdsec @v1.7.8 carries THREE GitHub advisories that OSV does not hold at
#     all -- not by package, not by the pinned version's commit, and not by id
#     (`GET https://api.osv.dev/v1/vulns/<ghsa>` returns `code 5` for each).
#     None has a CVE id, the same pattern T-0646 found for go-jose, so Trivy and
#     the image gate are blind to them too (T-0628) and a green gate is not
#     evidence here. All three are present-but-NOT-LINKED, measured on the
#     published binary and on the module graph rather than assumed (T-0653):
#       GHSA-rh69-4vqj-9gj8  medium  <= 1.7.8   fixed 1.8.0  kubernetes-audit
#                            acquisition webhook, unbounded request-body read
#       GHSA-g2x2-jgfg-pg7g  medium  <= 1.7.8   fixed 1.8.0  HTTP acquisition
#                            datasource, no decompressed-body cap, trusts
#                            Content-Length
#       GHSA-g9hc-g4w6-4mfx  low     <= 1.8.1   NO FIX YET   hardcoded
#                            InsecureSkipVerify in the notification-splunk
#                            plugin (cmd/notification-splunk/main.go:116)
#     This image pins `crowdsec/pkg/apiclient` -- the LAPI *client*, which is
#     what caddy-crowdsec-bouncer talks to. The whole transitive package
#     closure of that pin plus the bouncer is six packages: pkg/apiclient,
#     pkg/apiclient/useragent, pkg/fflag, pkg/models, pkg/modelscapi and
#     pkg/types. `go mod why` answers "main module does not need package" for
#     pkg/acquisition, both affected acquisition modules AND pkg/csplugin, and
#     `strings` on the binary from :stable finds 0 refs to any of them (against
#     456 for pkg/models as a control). The two acquisition bugs are how a
#     crowdsec AGENT ingests logs; this image runs no agent. The splunk one is
#     `package main` in cmd/, a standalone plugin binary Go cannot link into
#     another program even in principle -- so its missing fix cannot bind here.
#     Therefore do NOT raise this floor for these three: v1.8.0 would clear two
#     of them and v1.8.2 still would not clear the splunk one, while buying no
#     reachable fix and risking the build for a bouncer that needs 1.7.x.
#     A FOURTH row, GHSA-273h-gvwr-c3qj (CVE-2026-44981, LAPI unbounded gzip
#     decompression), is already cleared: its range is >= 1.7.0 patched in
#     1.7.8, i.e. exactly this pin. It still shows in the weekly report because
#     the range filter only drops a row on a demonstrable upper bound and that
#     range leads with `>=`. Conservative by design, not a bug.
#     Re-check if the bouncer ever links acquisition or notification code, or
#     if the splunk advisory gets a fixed release (T-0661).
#   - chi/v5 @v5.5.0 carries SEVEN GitHub advisories on the go-chi/chi repo.
#     FOUR survive this pin's range filter and recur in the weekly report as
#     "GitHub-only" rows; they are listed here so a reader who checks this pin
#     finds the answer instead of re-deriving it every week. NONE is linked.
#     Measured, not assumed (T-0652, re-measured T-0655, PoC re-run 2026-11-12
#     against v5.5.0, report re-run same day):
#       GHSA-mqqf-5wvp-8fh8  medium  >=5.2.2      NO FIX   open redirect in
#                            middleware.RedirectSlashes -- see the FALSE FIX
#                            note below, which is the important part
#       GHSA-3fxj-6jh8-hvhx  medium  >= 5.2.1     NO FIX   IP spoofing in
#                            middleware.RealIP
#       GHSA-9g5q-2w5x-hmxf  medium  since 0.9.0  NO FIX   IP spoofing via
#                            X-Forwarded-For in Request.RemoteAddr
#       GHSA-vrw8-fxc6-2r93  medium  v5.2.1       -        host-header
#                            injection -> open redirect in RedirectSlashes.
#                            Bounded below this pin, but the range is a BARE
#                            VERSION with no comparator, which the textual
#                            filter cannot prove excludes 5.5.0, so it is
#                            reported. Conservative by design, as with the
#                            crowdsec `>=` row above.
#     Two more are correctly filtered out and should NOT be expected in the
#     report: GHSA-rqrp-8cg2-5958 (<=5.4.0, = CVE-2026-118681, SupressNotFound
#     auth bypass) is genuinely CLEARED by this pin -- it is the advisory
#     v5.5.0 was taken for -- and GHSA-rjr7-jggh-pgcp (<=5.2.1) and
#     GHSA-j6qf-3m9c-vwj3 (<= 5.3.2) are bounded below it.
#     EVERY ONE of these lives in go-chi/chi/v5/middleware. That package is
#     NOT linked into this image: chi's core router is (105 string refs in the
#     v2.68.11 candidate binary, against 4250 for caddyserver/caddy/v2 as a
#     control) because Caddy uses it in caddypki/acmeserver and
#     smallstep/certificates imports it in acme/api, api, scep/api and
#     authority/admin/api -- but the middleware package has ZERO refs and the
#     symbols RedirectSlashes and SupressNotFound do not appear at all. The
#     binary is stripped, so `go tool nm` reports no symbols for anything
#     including Caddy itself; these counts come from `strings` with
#     known-present modules as controls, because the nm reading looks like a
#     clean answer while measuring nothing. So no middleware advisory is
#     reachable here and this floor is prophylactic -- worth keeping, because
#     smallstep/certificates ca/ca.go already imports chi/v5/middleware (for
#     GetHead only, never SupressNotFound) and that `ca` package is one import
#     away from being linked; the floor makes such a build take a fixed chi.
#     Note this is the IMAGE. The CaddyUI server binary is a separate program
#     that DOES import chi/v5/middleware (internal/server/server.go), but it
#     mounts only RequestLogger and Recoverer -- neither RedirectSlashes nor
#     SupressNotFound appears anywhere in this repo's Go source. Re-check that
#     if either is ever mounted.
#     *** THE CHI GATE IS A KNOWN-FALSE GREEN -- do not read it as clean. ***
#     GHSA-mqqf-5wvp-8fh8 covers TWO CVEs under one id: `gh api
#     /advisories/GHSA-mqqf-5wvp-8fh8` gives cve_id CVE-2025-69725 with
#     `>= 5.2.2, < 5.2.4`. v5.2.4 fixed the BACKSLASH variant; CVE-2026-122132
#     is the TAB bypass OF that fix, and GitHub kept only the first boundary.
#     OSV mirrors that truncated range (`introduced 5.2.2, fixed 5.2.4`), so
#     WHICH OSV QUESTION YOU ASK DECIDES THE ANSWER -- measured 2026-11-12:
#       package+version  chi/v5 @5.5.0            -> 0 vulns   (FALSE CLEAN)
#       by the tag's commit 3a33ba578e0d          -> CVE-2026-122132
#     The package+version query is the one an SBOM scanner asks, so Trivy and
#     caddy-image-gate.sh report chi clean. check-pinned-modules.sh asks BOTH
#     -- that second, commit-scoped query is exactly why this pin still shows
#     an advisory in the weekly report when Trivy shows none, and the report is
#     the one that is right. Do not "reconcile" them by dropping the commit
#     query. The standalone CVE-2026-122132 OSV record (package field null,
#     GIT range, 9 affected versions) and NVD both still list 5.2.2 through
#     5.5.0 affected, and the TAB bypass is live on the newest tag: running
#     scripts/poc/redirectslashes-tab.go (usage in its header) against v5.5.0 returns
#     `Location: "/\t/evil.com"` for a request to `/%09/evil.com/` (301).
#     RedirectSlashes is byte-identical between v5.2.4 and v5.5.0. A browser
#     strips the TAB, resolves //evil.com and follows it off-site.
#     This is strictly stronger than the T-0628 / T-0646 "OSV is blind to
#     CVE-less advisories" caveat: there the index held nothing, here it holds
#     a positive clean answer that is false. Settle it with the PoC, not with a
#     version query. There is no fixed tag to bump to; go-chi/chi#1238 is open.
#     Re-check when a tag lands that makes that PoC print no raw TAB (T-0664).
#   - caddy-crowdsec-bouncer is back on a tag. It was pinned to raw commit
#     bdd7e516 because it was the only upstream code supporting CrowdSec 1.7
#     (v0.14.1, the newest tag then, needed 1.6). v0.15.0 (2026-10-30) requires
#     crowdsec v1.8.1, so that reason is gone and an ordinary version comparison
#     works again. This tag is also upstream main's HEAD, so the pin is current
#     as well as legible. It is what actually sets the crowdsec version (1.8.1),
#     above the floor near the top of this block.
#   - EVERY --with below carries a version. An unpinned --with re-resolves to
#     whatever is latest at build time, so a base-layer refresh (an Alpine CVE,
#     a -pN tag) could pull new third-party code into the binary on all six
#     boxes — including a DNS provider holding TLS-cert API credentials and the
#     Coraza WAF. caddy-image-gate.sh blocks such a build from publishing by
#     diffing `caddy list-modules --versions`, but that is detection after the
#     fact: it stops the refresh rather than the drift, so an unrelated plugin
#     release holds the CVE fix hostage until a human investigates. Pinning
#     makes a plugin bump a deliberate edit, reviewed and versioned like any
#     other change, and leaves the gate as a second, independent check.
#     The trade, accepted knowingly: plugin fixes no longer arrive on their own
#     and need a deliberate bump here — the same trade already made for
#     crowdsec, x/net and chi.
#     caddy-dns/digitalocean has never been tagged upstream, so its pin is the
#     pseudo-version of master@04bde286 (2025-06-06) — the exact commit the
#     published image already contains, not a new choice.
#   - klauspost/compress @v1.20.4 lifts the module past GO-2026-6865 /
#     CVE-2026-116591 / GHSA-fpmh-frq8-7rj8 (HIGH, CWE-190): a crafted ~27 KB
#     two-block zstd frame panics DecodeAll and the streaming decoder with an
#     out-of-range slice index instead of returning a decode error. Vulnerable
#     <= v1.20.1, patched v1.20.2. The published image shipped v1.20.1.
#     THIS PIN IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT SLACK. MVS resolves to exactly v1.20.1 on
#     its own -- caddy 2.11.9, caddy-l4 v0.1.3, caddy-crowdsec-bouncer v0.15.0
#     and prometheus/client_golang v1.25.0 each require v1.20.1 and nothing in
#     the graph requires higher -- so without this line the build stays on the
#     vulnerable version. Unlike the crowdsec floor above, removing it
#     regresses the image. The module ROOT carries a Go package
#     (compressible.go), so a plain `--with <module>@ver` links and no
#     `--replace` is needed.
#     TRIVY IS BLIND TO THIS ONE, and NOT for the CVE-less reason seen with
#     go-jose and crowdsec -- this advisory HAS a CVE id and IS in OSV. The
#     cause is a package-vs-module NAMING MISMATCH: OSV/GHSA name the affected
#     package `github.com/klauspost/compress/zstd` (the subpackage), while Go
#     binaries and go.mod record the MODULE `github.com/klauspost/compress`.
#     Trivy matches the recorded name, finds no advisory under the module path
#     and reports nothing -- while still listing the package at v1.20.1 in its
#     own Packages output. Measured: a probe go.mod requiring the module gives
#     0 findings and one requiring the zstd subpackage path gives the HIGH,
#     same scanner and same DB. govulncheck is unaffected because the Go
#     advisory names the module correctly and matches on symbols. So a THIRD
#     distinct mechanism can hide a finding from Trivy, after CVE-less
#     advisories (T-0646) and `--replace` invisibility (T-0710): a scanner's
#     silence is not evidence (T-0729).
#     Reachability, re-derived against the ARTIFACT rather than a hand-built
#     probe -- and that distinction mattered here. The build runs with
#     `-tags nobadger,nomysql,nopgx` (recorded in the binary's build info), so
#     dgraph-io/badger is NOT LINKED AT ALL: 0 strings for `dgraph-io/badger`
#     in the shipped binary. A first probe that omitted those tags and most
#     --with plugins produced a confident but WRONG badger/smallstep-nosql
#     route; a probe rebuilt with all 13 plugins, `modules/standard` and the
#     real tags reproduces the binary's klauspost package set exactly. Use
#     `go mod why -vendor` as well: WITHOUT -vendor it reports a prometheus
#     promhttp route that runs through `prometheus/client_golang/
#     prometheus.test` and is TEST-ONLY.
#     The real non-test importers are Caddy's OWN
#     modules/caddyhttp/encode/zstd and DeRuina/timberjack (log rotation) --
#     so this IS the HTTP compression stack.
#     IT IS STILL NOT REACHABLE WITH HOSTILE INPUT, for a sharper reason:
#     GO-2026-6865 is a DECODER bug, and Caddy's encode/zstd is ENCODER-ONLY.
#     zstd.go calls zstd.NewWriter and nothing else; zstd_precompressed.go
#     merely declares the `.zst` suffix so the file server can serve
#     pre-compressed files as-is. Caddy compresses RESPONSES; it never
#     decompresses a zstd REQUEST body. A sweep of the whole 1274-package
#     shipped closure for non-test `zstd.NewReader` / `.DecodeAll(` finds hits
#     only inside klauspost/compress/zstd itself, with a NEGATIVE CONTROL
#     confirming the same sweep DOES fire for zstd.NewWriter (on encode/zstd
#     and timberjack) -- so the silence is a measurement, not a broken scan.
#     govulncheck names fse.Compress/Decompress and huff0.Compress1X/
#     Compress4X/ReadTable/EstimateSizes/AppendTable as reachable because they
#     are present and linked; the *Compress* ones are genuinely live via the
#     encoder. So `reachable` here means LINKED, not EXPLOITABLE.
#     Bumped anyway: the fix is free, the pin is cheap, and any future config
#     or plugin that decodes zstd would make it live.
#     v1.20.4 (not the minimum v1.20.2) because v1.20.2/.3/.4 are all clean at
#     both the module and zstd-subpackage paths in OSV, repo-level GitHub
#     advisories show only two ever (both fixed at or below v1.20.2), and
#     v1.20.4 is the current release.
#     THE ASSERTION BELOW IS DELIBERATELY NARROW. It accepts only v1.20.x for
#     x >= 4, and the trailing [[:space:]] matters: without it a PRE-RELEASE
#     pseudo-version such as v1.20.4-0.2026... would match while sorting BELOW
#     v1.20.4. So a future legitimate bump to v1.21.0 or beyond FAILS THE
#     BUILD until this pattern is widened. That is intended -- it fails closed,
#     and re-taking the decision is the point -- but it is a build break to
#     expect, not a mystery. Widen the pattern in the same commit as the bump.
#   - A pin that is behind upstream ON PURPOSE carries a `# pin-watch: floor`
#     comment immediately above it, read by scripts/check-pinned-modules.sh.
#     Such a pin is reported under "Intentional floors" with its reason rather
#     than counted as behind, so a decision that has already been taken stops
#     producing an `attention` verdict on every weekly run — a signal that is
#     always on carries no information (T-0651). It is an ACKNOWLEDGEMENT, not
#     an exclusion: `pin=`, `against=` and `reason=` are all required, the
#     acknowledgement expires by itself once upstream moves past `against=`,
#     an advisory on the pinned version always outranks it, and a directive
#     whose `pin=` no longer matches the line below it is a hard error rather
#     than a floor quietly inherited by a new version. So re-pinning a floored
#     module means re-taking the decision, which is the point.
FROM --platform=$BUILDPLATFORM caddy:2.11.9-builder AS builder
ARG TARGETOS=linux
ARG TARGETARCH
#   - The TWO --replace lines below remove github.com/go-jose/go-jose/v3 from
#     the binary entirely. That line had EIGHT advisories applying to our v3.0.5
#     with `first_patched_version: null` on every one -- 1 HIGH
#     (GHSA-mgcc-cww5-fp2q / CVE-2026-116587), 5 medium, 2 low -- so there was
#     never a v3 version to bump to. It was the last HIGH finding on this image.
#
#     WHY --replace AND NOT --with. go-jose/v3 is pulled in by
#     go.step.sm/crypto/jose, reached through smallstep/certificates under
#     Caddy's modules/caddypki -- the BASE PKI app, so it is linked by
#     `tls internal` too, not only by `acme_server`. Fixing it means raising
#     BOTH certificates and crypto together, and neither has an importable root
#     package, so `--with` fails them with
#     `build constraints exclude all Go files` -- xcaddy adds a blank import for
#     every --with, which only works for a package. `--replace` writes the
#     replace directive and no import, which is exactly what a library version
#     override needs. Measured: `--with` for these two does not build; the two
#     lines below do.
#
#     WHY BOTH, AND WHY THE PSEUDO-VERSION. go.step.sm/crypto v0.92.0 is the
#     release that migrates to go-jose v4 and drops v3, but it is an API BREAK:
#     certificates v0.30.2 -- the version Caddy 2.11.9 pins -- does not compile
#     against it (jwt.Builder has no CompactSerialize; jose.Expected has no
#     Audience, in authority/provisioner/{aws,azure,oidc}.go). So certificates
#     must move forward in the same build. Upstream has done the work but has
#     NOT TAGGED it: `certificates` @latest is still v0.30.2 (2026-03-19). The
#     pseudo-version below is upstream merge commit 6d5a251fbe01 (2026-11-05,
#     "Merge pull request #2848 from smallstep/mariano/crypto"), which is an
#     ancestor of smallstep/certificates master (behind_by 0) and is the EXACT
#     commit caddyserver/caddy master pins. So this is not a fork and not our
#     choice of commit -- it is Caddy's own, adopted ahead of a Caddy release.
#
#     RETIRE THESE TWO LINES once a Caddy release ships without a v3 line:
#       curl -s https://proxy.golang.org/github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2/@v/<ver>.mod | grep go-jose
#     When that prints no v3 line, both --replace lines become redundant and
#     should be deleted rather than re-pinned. Until then they are what keeps
#     v3 out. Verify on the artifact, never on this file:
#       go version -m /usr/bin/caddy | grep go-jose    # only a v4 line
#     Measured on this build: v3 absent from the binary AND from the import
#     closure (`go list -deps`), `caddy list-modules` identical at 194 modules
#     (136 standard + 58 non-standard; `wc -l` of that command reads 199 because
#     it counts two section headers and two blank lines, so re-measure by
#     summing the two totals, not by counting output lines)
#     vs the unreplaced build, no downgrades, and `tls internal` + the
#     acme_server directory verified working against a control build.
#
#     THE DOWNGRADE GUARD DOES NOT COVER --replace, so there is a second check
#     after it. `go: downgraded` is printed only by `go get`; xcaddy applies
#     replaces with `go mod edit -replace`, a pure text edit that does no MVS
#     comparison and prints nothing. Measured: `go get x@v0.20.0` then
#     `go mod edit -replace x=x@v0.3.8` leaves the module at v0.3.8 with ZERO
#     `go: downgraded` lines, so the existing guard passes. It still fully
#     protects every --with pin; it is blind to the two lines above. The added
#     check asserts the OUTCOME instead of the mechanism -- v3 absent from the
#     built binary -- which catches a silent lowering, a typo in either pin, and
#     a future upstream change that reintroduces v3, in one test. It is also the
#     exact close condition this work was given, enforced at build time rather
#     than left to a reviewer.
#
#     WRITE THE MODULE LIST TO A FILE FIRST. Do not rewrite this as
#     `go version -m /usr/bin/caddy | grep -q ...`. That is how the check was
#     first written here and it was VACUOUSLY TRUE: `grep -q` exits on its
#     first match, `go version -m` then dies on SIGPIPE, and under
#     `set -o pipefail` (head of this RUN) the pipeline returns 141 -- which
#     `if` reads as "no match". Measured on a binary that DOES link v3:
#     `grep -c` found it while `grep -q` returned 141 and the build passed.
#     Redirecting to a file first also makes a failed `go version -m` abort the
#     `&&` chain, so a missing or unreadable binary fails CLOSED -- which
#     `... | grep -q ... || true` would not do, since `|| true` masks exactly
#     the case the guard exists for.
#     The second `if` is a liveness check: it fails unless a v4 line is present,
#     so an empty module list cannot make the v3 test pass by having nothing to
#     match. Verified against three inputs inside this builder image: v3-free
#     binary -> 0, v3-linked binary -> 1 with the message, missing binary -> 1.
#     The NEGATIVE CONTROL is what caught the original defect, and is how to
#     check these lines after touching them: build with the two --replace lines
#     removed and this step MUST fail. With `grep -q` it did not. A guard that
#     cannot go red is not a guard.
#
#     NOTE FOR THE PIN WATCHER: scripts/check-pinned-modules.sh parses --with
#     only, so these two lines are NOT watched -- see T-0710.
#
#     BEFORE ADDING A --with UNDER EITHER REPLACED MODULE PATH: xcaddy SILENTLY
#     SKIPS IT. environment.go:226-230 (v0.4.7) tests each plugin package path
#     against every replaced module with `strings.HasPrefix(p.PackagePath,
#     repl+"/")` and does `continue nextPlugin`, so `go get` never runs for it
#     -- no error, no log line, and the requested version is simply not applied.
#     That makes a pin look present in this file while having no effect, which
#     is the same shape as the vacuous-grep defect described above. Checked at
#     the time of writing: all 16 --with paths below, and none is prefixed by
#     `github.com/smallstep/certificates/` or `go.step.sm/crypto/`. Re-run that
#     comparison when adding a --with; e.g. `--with go.step.sm/crypto/foo@vX`
#     would be accepted here and then ignored.
RUN set -o pipefail && GOOS=${TARGETOS} GOARCH=${TARGETARCH} xcaddy build \
    --replace github.com/smallstep/certificates=github.com/smallstep/certificates@v0.30.3-0.20261105213751-6d5a251fbe01 \
    --replace go.step.sm/crypto=go.step.sm/crypto@v0.92.1 \
    # pin-watch: floor pin=v1.8.1 against=v1.8.2 ref=T-0753 reason=v1.7.8 was the exact version fixing CVE-2026-44982 and CVE-2026-44981, so it was a true CVE-BEARING boundary -- but NOT a clean version: 2 of the 3 GitHub-only advisories reach <= 1.7.8, and OSV is blind to all 3 because none carries a CVE id, so an OSV-clean reading of either version proves nothing (T-0646). All 3 are acquisition/notification code, outside the 6-package closure of pkg/apiclient + the bouncer, so none is linked and a bump buys no reachable fix; v1.8.2 still would not clear the splunk one. RAISED v1.7.8 -> v1.8.1 BY T-0753, which is a correction to the written pin and not a change to what ships: the bouncer at v0.15.0 requires crowdsec v1.8.1, MVS has resolved to 1.8.1 since T-0636, and the published binary has carried 1.8.1 all along. The floor is written at the version actually linked so the Dockerfile stops disagreeing with its own artefact -- the two acquisition advisories past v1.7.8 were cleared in fact, and are now cleared on paper too. Still below upstream v1.8.2, so this stays a floor and stays exempt from staleness. Kept so a future bouncer cannot silently drop below it. Measured: header, T-0653, T-0816.
    --with github.com/crowdsecurity/crowdsec/pkg/apiclient@v1.8.1 \
    # pin-watch: security pin=v0.62.0 ref=T-0667 reason=lifts golang.org/x/net past CVE-2026-78669, CVE-2026-78661 and CVE-2026-97045. Asserted against the built binary by scripts/assert-floor-versions.sh: a later plugin raising this via MVS is a change to a reviewed security decision and must be seen, not absorbed.
    --with golang.org/x/net/http2@v0.62.0 \
    # pin-watch: security pin=v5.5.0 ref=T-0652 reason=lifts go-chi past CVE-2026-114437 and CVE-2026-118681; CVE-2026-122132 has no fixed tag yet. chi itself IS linked (caddypki/acmeserver, smallstep acme/api) but chi/v5/middleware is not, so this floor is prophylactic -- kept because smallstep ca/ca.go already imports that package.
    --with github.com/go-chi/chi/v5@v5.5.0 \
    # pin-watch: security pin=v4.1.9 ref=T-0753 reason=lifts go-jose past SIX live advisories: CVE-2026-116587 plus five CVE-less ones (v6h2, c67m, p9j4, r39w, 27pc) each vulnerable at <= v4.1.6 and patched only in v4.1.7. 4.1.6 would be insufficient. Five are absent from OSV and from GitHub's global index, so an OSV-clean reading proves nothing here. WRITTEN AT v4.1.9, NOT v4.1.7, BY T-0753: the --replace on go.step.sm/crypto (T-0718; v0.92.0, and v0.92.1 since T-0854 -- both go.mod files say so) requires go-jose/v4 v4.1.9, so MVS has shipped v4.1.9 since that landed while this line still said v4.1.7 -- a divergence nothing reported until T-0816 asserted the pins against the binary. v4.1.9 is current upstream and strictly past the v4.1.7 fix, so the security claim above is unchanged and merely stated at the version that actually ships. Raising it is required for --strict on the publish paths, which otherwise fails every release on an already-reviewed lift.
    --with github.com/go-jose/go-jose/v4@v4.1.9 \
    # pin-watch: security pin=v1.59.4 ref=I182 reason=lifts aws-sdk-go-v2/service/route53 past GHSA-3jcv-796g-cpjg (low, no CVE id: the SDK never validated that the configured AWS region is a valid host label before building the endpoint URL, so a bad region value could send API calls to a non-AWS host). The route53 DNS plugin pinned below stays at v1.6.2 (see its floor), and that pulls in service/route53 v1.58.3, which is inside the advisory range (< v1.59.4). The fixed code IS linked: route53.bindRegion and bindEndpointParams are both in the shipped binary. It is low-risk in practice because the region comes from the admin's own Caddy config, and route53 is not configured anywhere in the fleet (see the caddy-dns/route53 floor). v1.59.4 is the exact fixed version, not latest (v1.73.1), on purpose. Its go.mod asks only for aws-sdk-go-v2 v1.39.6, smithy-go v1.23.2, configsources v1.4.13 and endpoints/v2 v2.7.13, all lower than what this image already links, so MVS moves this one module and nothing else. The source diff v1.58.3..v1.59.4 is the region check plus two new us-isob-west-1 enum values and doc comments. GitHub's per-repository index is the only place this advisory appears (it has no CVE id). The watcher's "< v1.108.8" range for it on the aws-sdk-go-v2 umbrella module is service/rds's boundary misattributed (T-0889). EXPECT the watcher to show CVE-2026-89090 against THIS pin, and do not treat it as real. OSV's CVE-2026-89090 record is a GIT range over the whole aws-sdk-go-v2 monorepo (fixed at commit ef3322b8, 2026-03-23), so a commit-scoped query matches any SDK module tagged before that date. Its only vulnerable package is aws/protocol/eventstream, which is absent from both the module graph and the binary (0 strings). The package+version OSV query for service/route53 is clean at v1.58.3, v1.59.4 and v1.73.1. The first route53 tag past that commit is v1.62.5 (also safe by MVS), but it brings about 4.8k lines of generated SDK churn. That trade is the release owner's call, not this floor's. Mark this `security`, not `floor`: it is a minimum, and the weekly watcher should still say it is behind upstream.
    --with github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go-v2/service/route53@v1.59.4 \
    --with github.com/klauspost/compress@v1.20.4 \
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/cloudflare@v0.2.4 \
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/porkbun@v0.3.1 \
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/namecheap@v1.0.0 \
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/godaddy@v1.2.0 \
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/digitalocean@v0.0.0-20250606074528-04bde2867106 \
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/hetzner/v2@v2.0.1 \
    # pin-watch: floor pin=v1.6.2 against=v1.6.3 ref=T-0637 reason=route53 is configured on ZERO boxes -- re-derived 2026-11-13 by reading the RUNNING config (127.0.0.1:2019/config/) inside the caddy container on all six hosts, six distinct config sizes, 0 route53 references on every one; the fleet is Cloudflare-only for DNS-01. OSV is clean on BOTH sides (v1.6.2 pinned and v1.6.3), so staying accrues no known exposure and moving buys no reachable fix. 7 of the 8 commits in v1.6.2..v1.6.3 are dependabot/CI housekeeping; the only functional one drags aws-sdk-go-v2 1.39.1->1.48.0, service/route53 1.58.3->1.70.10, smithy-go 1.23.0->1.28.9, ADDS service/signin and DROPS internal/ini. That module-set change cannot ride inside a release whose verified claim is "module set membership did not change", and libdns/route53 v1.6.3 alters DeleteRecords wildcard semantics in a credentialed DNS provider, so it needs a release whose review scope covers a credentialed DNS provider rather than any release merely in flight (T-0625 declined it on exactly that ground and was right).
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/route53@v1.6.2 \
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/gandi@v1.2.1 \
    --with github.com/hslatman/caddy-crowdsec-bouncer/http@v0.15.0 \
    --with github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit@v0.1.0 \
    # BEFORE MOVING THIS PIN: run `./scripts/layer4-concurrency/run.sh --prove`
    # against the candidate image and record the result in the bump's card.
    # Not a `pin-watch:` directive on purpose -- this is not a floor (v0.1.3 IS
    # upstream latest), and check-pinned-modules.sh errors on any directive kind
    # other than `floor`, so a made-up kind would break the image gate.
    # v0.1.3's two headline fixes (#483 buffer reuse, #482/#492 reload deadlock)
    # live on the listener-wrapper path, which the release e2e suite CANNOT
    # reach: it drives a dedicated layer4 server with sequential single
    # connections, so the hijacked path never executes. Both were verified for
    # that release by READING THE SOURCE DIFF -- a bump that regresses either one
    # would therefore pass every check in this repo. That is exactly how #483
    # reached a release upstream. Takes ~4 min and needs docker plus the
    # previous image for the differential. Avoid shell operator characters in
    # this block -- check-pinned-modules.sh prints comment lines into the pin
    # region before filtering them, so a stray one could truncate it.
    # `--prove` is not optional: a run that does not ALSO fail
    # on the pre-fix image establishes nothing, and this harness caught itself
    # passing on the buggy image twice while being built (T-0631, T-0714).
    --with github.com/mholt/caddy-l4@v0.1.3 \
    --with github.com/corazawaf/coraza-caddy/v2@v2.6.2 \
    # SECURITY FLOOR (T-0840): lifts go.opentelemetry.io/otel/sdk/log past GO-2026-6974 /
    # CVE-2026-128085 (AttributeValueLengthLimit bypass via U+FFFD runes) -- the only REACHABLE
    # advisory left on this image. Caddy 2.11.9 requires sdk/log@v0.22.0 and NO plugin asks for
    # more, so without this floor MVS selects v0.22.0 and the finding stands.
    # Do NOT read the advisory's "fixed in v1.47.0" as a bump of otel itself: it names three
    # modules, and otel/sdk and otel/trace were ALREADY at the fixed v1.47.0 in the published
    # image, so sdk/log was the whole residual. v0.22.0 -> v1.47.0 is a v0-to-v1 graduation at
    # the SAME import path (no /v2), so it is not an API break -- measured: build clean, no
    # `go: downgraded`, `caddy list-modules --versions` = 199, identical to the control.
    # Unlike the klauspost case this one is NOT Trivy-blind (Trivy: CVE-2026-128085 MEDIUM).
    # Marked `security`, not `floor` (T-0905): it is AT current upstream, not deliberately
    # behind, so the weekly watcher still reports it if upstream moves past it, while
    # assert-floor-versions.sh asserts it against the BUILT BINARY like every other security pin.
    # pin-watch: security pin=v1.47.0 ref=T-0840 reason=lifts go.opentelemetry.io/otel/sdk/log past GO-2026-6974 / CVE-2026-128085 (AttributeValueLengthLimit bypass via U+FFFD runes), the only REACHABLE advisory left on this image. Caddy 2.11.9 requires sdk/log@v0.22.0 and no plugin asks for more, so without this floor MVS selects v0.22.0 and the finding stands. v0.22.0 -> v1.47.0 is a v0-to-v1 graduation at the same import path, measured clean (no downgrade, 199 modules). Full reasoning in the comment above.
    --with go.opentelemetry.io/otel/sdk/log@v1.47.0 \
    # SECURITY FLOOR (T-0840), PROPHYLACTIC: lifts golang.org/x/crypto past GO-2026-5932 (the
    # openpgp package is unmaintained and unsafe by design). This buys no REACHABLE fix --
    # openpgp is NOT LINKED here. The advisory is PACKAGE-scoped with no `symbols` key on any
    # imports entry, which is why govulncheck renders its 7 "vulnerable symbols" as package globs
    # rather than function names, and its "fix" version is a deprecation commit
    # (go.dev/issue/81227) not a code change. ALL SEVEN of those paths were enumerated from OSV
    # and counted individually in the shipped binary rather than inferred from one prefix grep,
    # because a prefix only covers them if every glob sits under that subtree. They all do, and
    # each is absent:
    #     x/crypto/openpgp 0, openpgp/packet 0, openpgp/armor 0, openpgp/clearsign 0,
    #     openpgp/errors 0, openpgp/elgamal 0, openpgp/s2k 0
    # against working positive controls in the SAME module, which is what makes a zero mean
    # anything: x/crypto/ssh 226, chacha20 64, bcrypt 20, argon2 20, hkdf 4.
    # The module ships; the vulnerable package is not compiled in.
    # Pinned anyway because the floor is FREE: v0.59.0 is a plain TAG above the advisory's
    # pseudo-version fix, OSV-clean, needs no --replace, and holds the module set at 199 with no
    # downgrade. It also removes a standing UNKNOWN-severity row from the gate's Trivy view.
    # Spelled as the importable subpackage `x/crypto/chacha20` because xcaddy adds a blank import
    # per --with and the module root `golang.org/x/crypto` is not an importable package -- the
    # same reason the x/net pin above names x/net/http2. A root-path spelling FAILS the build
    # with "no required module provides package golang.org/x/crypto" (measured).
    # assert-floor-versions.sh walks the package path up to its module, exactly as it does for
    # x/net/http2, so the floor is asserted against golang.org/x/crypto in the built binary (T-0905).
    # pin-watch: security pin=v0.59.0 ref=T-0840 reason=PROPHYLACTIC: lifts golang.org/x/crypto past GO-2026-5932 (openpgp, unmaintained and unsafe by design). Buys no reachable fix -- all seven openpgp package paths are absent from the shipped binary against working positive controls in the same module -- but the floor is free: a plain tag above the advisory's pseudo-version fix, OSV-clean, no --replace, module set held at 199 with no downgrade, and it removes a standing UNKNOWN-severity Trivy row. Full reasoning in the comment above.
    --with golang.org/x/crypto/chacha20@v0.59.0 \
    2>&1 | tee /tmp/xcaddy.log \
    && if grep 'go: downgraded' /tmp/xcaddy.log; then echo 'a pin downgraded a module' >&2; exit 1; fi \
    && go version -m /usr/bin/caddy > /tmp/mods.txt \
    && if ! grep -qE '^[[:space:]]*dep[[:space:]]+github\.com/klauspost/compress[[:space:]]+v1\.20\.(4|[5-9]|[1-9][0-9])[[:space:]]' /tmp/mods.txt; then \
         echo 'klauspost/compress is not >= v1.20.4 in the built binary (GO-2026-6865)' >&2; \
         grep -E 'klauspost/compress' /tmp/mods.txt >&2 || true; exit 1; \
       fi \
    && if grep -q 'go-jose/go-jose/v3' /tmp/mods.txt; then \
         echo 'go-jose/v3 is linked again: the --replace pins above no longer remove it' >&2; exit 1; \
       fi \
    && if ! grep -q 'go-jose/go-jose/v4' /tmp/mods.txt; then \
         echo 'no go-jose/v4 line: the module list is unusable, so the v3 check above proved nothing' >&2; exit 1; \
       fi

# The OTHER half of the downgrade guard above (T-0667).
#
# That grep catches a pin being pulled DOWN. It cannot see a plugin raising a
# floor PAST its pin, because `go: upgraded` is a different word, and a module
# merely being ADDED at a higher version prints no line at all. Measured: the
# `--with` list above builds clean, prints no `go: downgraded`, and ships
# crowdsec v1.8.1 against a v1.7.8 floor -- the Dockerfile says one version,
# the binary contains another, and nothing said so.
#
# So the pins are asserted against the ARTEFACT rather than the log. Below its
# pin fails the build; above it is printed as a named divergence, because a
# security pin is a statement about which code ships and a change to it should
# be reviewed, not absorbed.
#
# Run HERE rather than only in a workflow so it holds for every build path,
# including a local `docker build`: a check that lives in CI alone is absent
# from the build it describes. It needs `go`, which only this stage has.
#
# The Dockerfile is COPYed in because the directives ARE the pin list -- there
# is no second list to drift. Copied to /tmp rather than read in place because
# this stage has no working copy of the repo.
#
# COPY TAKES IT FROM THE BUILD CONTEXT, WHICH IS NOT NECESSARILY THE FILE
# BEING BUILT. `docker build -f Dockerfile.variant .` builds the variant but
# `COPY Dockerfile.caddy` still fetches the context's copy, so the assertion
# would read one file while the binary came from another -- and would report
# the CONTEXT's pins as satisfied no matter what the variant declared.
# Measured while adding this: a variant whose directive demanded crowdsec
# v1.9.0 built clean, exited 0 and produced a byte-identical image digest to
# the unmodified build, because the edited directive was never read. That is
# the exact fail-open shape this check exists to remove, reproduced inside
# the check itself.
#
# So the pins are re-derived from THIS build's own `--with` arguments: the
# ARGs below are set from the same lines above, and the assertion cross-checks
# them against the copied Dockerfile. A mismatch means the file read is not
# the file built, and that is a hard error rather than a pass. Keep
# FLOOR_SELFCHECK in step with the pinned versions above; the assertion fails
# the build if they drift, which is the point.
COPY scripts/assert-floor-versions.sh /tmp/assert-floor-versions.sh
COPY Dockerfile.caddy /tmp/Dockerfile.caddy
ARG FLOOR_SELFCHECK="github.com/crowdsecurity/crowdsec/pkg/apiclient@v1.8.1 golang.org/x/net/http2@v0.62.0 github.com/go-chi/chi/v5@v5.5.0 github.com/go-jose/go-jose/v4@v4.1.9 github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go-v2/service/route53@v1.59.4 github.com/caddy-dns/route53@v1.6.2 go.opentelemetry.io/otel/sdk/log@v1.47.0 golang.org/x/crypto/chacha20@v0.59.0"

# STRICT ON THE PUBLISH PATHS, REPORTING EVERYWHERE ELSE (T-0753).
#
# A floor linked BELOW its pin always fails the build; that is not a choice.
# Upward movement is the choice, and it is taken differently depending on what
# the build is for. A plugin raising a security floor through MVS makes the
# reasoning behind the pinned version stale by definition -- somebody chose
# that exact version for a reason -- and the moment to re-take that decision
# is before the image reaches Docker Hub and six edge proxies, not whenever a
# human next happens to read a green build's log.
#
# So release-docker.yml and caddy-image-refresh.yml pass FLOOR_STRICT=1 and a
# lift stops the pipeline until the pin and its reason are updated. A local
# `docker build` still only prints the divergence: the cost of the strict
# reading is that a plugin bump which moves a floor takes two commits, and
# paying that on a developer's iteration loop buys nothing, because a local
# build publishes nothing.
#
# THE FLAG IS DERIVED HERE, NOT INTERPOLATED, AND AN UNRECOGNISED VALUE IS A
# HARD ERROR. Writing `--expect "$FLOOR_SELFCHECK" $FLOOR_STRICT` would let an
# empty or misspelt build-arg produce a NON-STRICT PUBLISH in silence, which
# is the single outcome this must never do quietly -- a protection that can be
# lost to a typo is not one. `FLOOR_STRICT=stirct` therefore exits 2 rather
# than building the weaker way, and the workflows' values are asserted by
# scripts/test-floor-versions.sh so a publish path cannot drop the flag
# without a test failing.
# Hand-kept count of `# pin-watch: floor|security` directives above (T-0906).
# Fewer parsed directives than this fails the build even if --expect were
# dropped. Keep it in step with FLOOR_SELFCHECK.
ARG FLOOR_MIN=8
ARG FLOOR_STRICT=0
RUN set -e; \
    case "$FLOOR_STRICT" in \
      1|true|yes|on)   strict=--strict ;; \
      0|false|no|off) strict= ;; \
      *) echo "FLOOR_STRICT must be 0 or 1, got '$FLOOR_STRICT'; refusing to guess which reading was wanted" >&2; exit 2 ;; \
    esac; \
    sh /tmp/assert-floor-versions.sh \
      --binary /usr/bin/caddy \
      --dockerfile /tmp/Dockerfile.caddy \
      --expect "$FLOOR_SELFCHECK" \
      --min-floors "$FLOOR_MIN" \
      $strict

FROM caddy:2.11.9-alpine
# The caddy base image only gets Alpine fixes when it is rebuilt upstream;
# upgrade here so this image ships them (e.g. brotli-libs 1.2.1, CVE-2026-111497).
RUN apk upgrade --no-cache
COPY --from=builder /usr/bin/caddy /usr/bin/caddy
